%U A402329 4,0,1,0,8,8,2,2,1,5,2,1,7,0,4,0,8,4,4
%N A402329 Decimal expansion of Sum_{n>=1} sqrt(4*n^2+1)/2^(n+2).
%C A402329 Length of the polygonal line joining the origin and the points (2^(-m), m/2^(m-1)), m >= 2. These points lie on the curve y = -2*x*log_2(x), 0 < x <= 1/4; the segment between the points with m = n+2 and m = n+1 has length sqrt(4*n^2+1)/2^(n+2).
%C A402329 Equivalently, (1/4)*E[|1 + 2*N*i|] where N is a geometric random variable with P(N = n) = 1/2^n, n >= 1.
%C A402329 In the context of horizontal visibility graphs of time series, this polygonal is the limit of the partial visibility curves of the period-2^k orbits of the logistic map x -> r*x*(1-x) along the Feigenbaum period-doubling cascade, so this constant is the length of the partial visibility curve at the onset of chaos, r = A098587 (Nuño and Muñoz, 2019).
%D A402329 J. C. Nuño and F. J. Muñoz, The partial visibility curve of the Feigenbaum cascade to chaos. Chaos, Solitons & Fractals, 2020, Volume 131, 109537, ISSN 0960-0779.
%H A402329 Francisco J. Muñoz, <a href="/A402329/b402329.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A402329 J. C. Nuño and F. J. Muñoz, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.07322">The Partial Visibility Curve of the Feigenbaum Cascade to Chaos</a>, arXiv:1905.07322 [physics.data-an], 2019.
%F A402329 Equals Sum_{n>=1} sqrt(4*n^2+1)/2^(n+2).
%F A402329 Equals Sum_{n>=1} abs(1+2*n*i)/2^(n+2), i = sqrt(-1).
%e A402329 1.04143878639568172024754105532658596872842777596360656951850...
%t A402329 RealDigits[N[Sum[Sqrt[4 n^2 + 1]/2^(n + 2), {n, 1, 500}], 120], 10, 100][[1]]
%o A402329 (PARI) \p 120; suminf(n=1, sqrt(4*n^2+1)/2^(n+2))
%Y A402329 Cf. A098587, A006890.
